WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.440
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers, episode 63, from May 5th, 2026.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.820
The universe has been busy, let's get into it.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.200
Right now NASA and Ross Cosmos are jointly assessing fissures that have appeared in modules

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.280
of the International Space Station.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.860
These cracks stem from decades of thermal cycling and micrometeoroid impacts on the aging

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:25.380
structure.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:32.180
Engineers on both sides are analyzing stress patterns and evaluating whether targeted

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.600
repairs or operational adjustments can extend the outpost's life.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:43.560
On-going monitoring remains critical to preserving the station's long-term structural integrity.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.860
Continued cooperation is essential because the International Space Station is still the

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.080
only long-duration orbital laboratory available.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.840
The open question is how long the current modules can remain safe before major refurbishment

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:00.960
becomes necessary.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.760
The answers will shape how we keep a continuous human presence in orbit while newer stations

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:07.580
take shape.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.460
While partners focus on extending the life of our current orbital outpost, new commercial

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.560
rockets are preparing to expand access to space.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:24.160
Firefly Aerospace plans to debut its upgraded Alpha Block 2 rocket late the summer amid rising

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:24.780
demand.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:31.700
Strong demand, especially for national security payloads, is accelerating the Block 2 development.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:38.020
The upgraded configuration reflects the expanding needs of the commercial launch market where

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:42.040
more missions now require reliable and frequent access to orbit.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.660
The debut flight will test how the new systems perform once they leave the ground, giving

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:52.140
an early look at whether the changes deliver the cadence operators are counting on.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:58.040
As new rockets prepare to reach orbit, lunar landing hardware is also clearing key ground

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:58.620
tests.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:05.380
Blue Origin's Moon Lander has completed a series of tests inside a NASA vacuum chamber

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.120
that simulates lunar conditions.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:13.540
The successful campaign moves the vehicle closer to readiness for future surface operations.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.480
Engineers now have valuable data on how the lander handles the extreme environment it will

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:25.300
face on the moon, particularly the thermal and vacuum stresses that will define every

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.240
surface task.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:33.040
This milestone advances the overall program and brings surface operations one step closer.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:39.380
From the human side of exploration, we turn to a subtle but constant feature of our own

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:40.520
planet's atmosphere.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.500
Now here's something that blew my mind when I looked into it.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:51.240
Air Glow is produced when solar ultraviolet radiation splits oxygen and nitrogen molecules

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.140
high above the surface during daylight hours.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:59.800
And night, those atoms recombine and release the stored energy as faint photons.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.980
If every square centimeter of the night sky were gathered into a single point, air glow

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.820
would still outshine the combined light of all visible stars in certain wavelengths.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:17.180
The glow peaks near 90 kilometers altitude, where temperatures sit around negative 90

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.300
degrees Celsius and pressures are less than a millionth of sea level.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:28.080
This thin, ever-present shell circles the entire planet and changes in brightness with

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:34.980
solar activity, latitude and season, yet some wavelengths brighten or fade on time scales

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.440
that current chemistry cannot fully explain.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:43.400
Why do those particular changes still surprise researchers tracking the upper atmosphere?

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.900
The answers matter because air glow is one of the few ways we can watch global atmospheric

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.640
chemistry evolve in real time from the ground.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:59.240
While air glow offers a global view from above, new satellite imagery is revealing dynamic

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.100
features much closer to home.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:09.580
Fresh NASA satellite images highlight geothermal features around ahu-chapan and nearby peaks

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:10.560
in El Salvador.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:16.740
The observation support ongoing monitoring of the region, historically known as the lighthouse

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.620
of the Pacific.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.980
These data add to our understanding of active volcanic systems and how they behave over time,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:30.100
giving volcanologists another steady stream of surface temperature and gas emission clues.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:36.620
From Earth's dynamic surface, we shift to a small solar system body currently crossing

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:37.460
our night sky.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.820
The 10th magnitude asteroid, 13 Ageria, is moving through Virgo this month.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:50.340
Its relatively rapid motion allows amateur astronomers to follow it across the sky in just

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:56.480
a few hours, such sightings provide valuable practice in tracking faint solar system objects

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:03.000
against background stars, sharpening skills that come in handy when new near-earth asteroids

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.200
are discovered and need quick confirmation.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:11.700
Hands-on observing leads naturally to the kind of patient imaging that captures the changing night

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:12.560
sky itself.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:20.640
A time-blended panorama from Dendi Arons captures both the winter and summer Milky Way arches rising

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.540
above the peaks.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:28.160
The composite image shows how dramatically the night sky shifts over the course of a single night.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:34.460
The technique demonstrates creative ways to document celestial motion and the seasonal change

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:40.320
in our view of the galaxy, turning a long night of photography into a single frame that

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.240
records six months of Earth's orbit in one view.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:49.840
Next time we'll be watching for the first flight data once that upgraded alpha block

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.060
two rocket lifts off late this summer.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.920
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:06:01.740
If you enjoyed this, a rating or review on Apple podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:08.140
find the show. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, thanks for exploring with me, and I'll see you next time.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:15.100
